by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-23T07:37:07Z

Thanks for the report and for the patch. I reworked the mechanism: instead of creating the file and then chmod'ing it, the umask is forced around the `fopen()` call. Create-then-chmod only narrows the window, it does not close it. A second process polling the directory with plain `scandir()` still saw 1565 of 9520 files at `0644` before the chmod landed; with the umask forced, 4088 of 4088 were `0600` on first sight.

The chmod also broke a call that used to work: `self::box('chmod', ...)` goes through `assertFunctionExists()`, so with `-d disable_functions=chmod` a suffixed `tempnam()` went from returning a filename to throwing an `IOException`. And custom stream wrappers usually do not implement `stream_metadata`, so on those the chmod was a no-op and the file stayed at `0644`.

`umask()` is already an unguarded dependency of this class (`copy()` and `dumpFile()` both call it), and forcing it around a creation is what `Console\Input\File\InputFile` does for the same reason.

I also added an assertion on the umask value after the call, so a leaked umask fails the test.
